          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-c93b9ce1720ccfb5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.9,\"analysis\":\"Natasha directly avows her love for Princess Marya, strongly presenting that emotional state as true. The Russian degree-exclamative “Как я тебя люблю” (“How I love you”) and its characterization as an expression of feeling give moderate independent support to expressive force, although the utterance follows reflection and is not portrayed as an involuntary blurt. It participates in forming their intimacy, but the explicit relational undertaking belongs chiefly to the following proposal that they be friends, so performative support for the core utterance itself is low.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is whether the grammatical exclamative and narrator’s description establish independent expressive force or merely an emotionally intense avowal. There is also slight performative pressure from the immediately adjacent friendship proposal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Natasha directly addresses Princess Marya: “Как я тебя люблю” (“How I love you”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After shared bereavement and sustained mutual care, Natasha has been reflecting on Marya’s goodness and affection. Her timid avowal, embraces, and kisses initiate an exceptionally intense and tender friendship. The context supports a sincere, considered declaration with expressive outpouring and relational consequences, rather than deception, irony, or a purely reflexive accident.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Маша, голубушка. Как я тебя люблю.” (“Masha, darling. How I love you.”)\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense avowal strongly supports truth-conditional force; the degree-exclamative construction also provides some positive evidence of expressive force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Natasha thinks that Marya is special, possesses “everything good,” loves her, and is beautiful.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance emerges from conscious reflection on Marya, supporting a considered avowal and limiting the case for predominantly reflex-like production.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator calls Natasha’s embraces and kisses “этому выражению чувств Наташи” (“this expression of Natasha’s feelings”).\",\"supports\":\"Supports genuine affective expression and moderate expressive force rather than merely formal assertion.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Natasha immediately adds, “Будем совсем, совсем друзьями” (“Let us be completely, completely friends”).\",\"supports\":\"Shows a nearby explicit relational proposal, giving the love avowal limited performative coloring while locating the clearest undertaking in a separate sentence.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says that from that day a passionate and tender friendship arose between Natasha and Princess Marya.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the avowal’s sincere relational setting and consequences without making the core words themselves primarily performative.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal, E captures the independently supported exclamative expression of feeling, and low P registers limited relational enactment. No important aspect of the core utterance requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The passage presents intense female friendship and shared grief; it does not require interpreting the avowal as romantic or erotic.\"}"
